CloudBurrow patch 105 to github.com/ncruces/go-sqlite3-wasm/v2 v2.2.35301
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#2008). Applies to the module as published.

BigQuery tables have up to 10,000 columns
(https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/quotas, "Maximum number of columns
in a table"). The engine stores each BigQuery table as one SQLite table, and
this module's SQLite was compiled with SQLite's default SQLITE_MAX_COLUMN of
2,000, so CREATE TABLE of about 1,996 columns or more (the engine adds its
own stream-time and change-history columns) failed "too many columns".

This is the module's own build (build/build.sh, wasi-sdk 33, binaryen 129,
wasm2go v0.4.3, SQLite 3.53.1, as tools.sh and build/download.sh fetch them)
run with one line added to build/sqlite_opt.h: SQLITE_MAX_COLUMN 32767,
SQLite's largest allowed value
(https://www.sqlite.org/limits.html#max_column). Rebuilt without the line,
the same steps reproduce the published sqlite3.go, sqlite3.dat and libc.go
byte for byte (checked 2026-11-20 on darwin/arm64), so the hunks below and
the sqlite3.dat beside this patch are exactly what the define changes:
the hard column limit in the data segment, and the compile-option string
"MAX_COLUMN=32767", one byte longer than "MAX_COLUMN=2000", which moves the
data addresses after it. sqlite3.dat is binary, so sources.json copies it
from 0105-max-column-32767.sqlite3.dat (its sha256, which sources.json pins)
instead of patching it.

To rebuild: copy the module out of the module cache, apply the
build/sqlite_opt.h hunk, run tools.sh, build/download.sh and build/build.sh,
then diff sqlite3.go and compare sqlite3.dat. CloudBurrow enforces BigQuery's
own 10,000-column limit before the engine is reached.

SQLite is public domain and the module's own files are MIT-0 (MIT No
Attribution); CloudBurrow's change, the one-line define, is offered under
the MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 The CloudBurrow Authors.

